What is this project doing?
=============================
This project is mostly a demonstration of: Docker + Nginx + Go working together
Purpose: Learn how to containerize a Go web application and place Nginx in front of it as a reverse proxy.
"How a reverse proxy (Nginx) forwards requests to a backend service written in Go running inside a Docker container."

Your architecture is:
=========================
Browser
   |
   | Port 80
   v
+---------+
|  NGINX  |
+---------+
     |
     |
     v
+---------+
| Go App  |
| Backend |
+---------+

So:
➔ User opens browser
➔ Request reaches Nginx
➔ Nginx forwards request to Go backend
➔ Go backend responds


What is go.mod?
===============
Think of: go.mod as Go's equivalent of:

➔ package.json (Node.js)
➔ pom.xml (Maven)
➔ requirements.txt (Python)

It defines:
➔ Project name
➔ Go version
➔ Dependencies

What is go.sum?
===============
Think of: "go.sum" as the lock file.

Equivalent to:
package-lock.json
yarn.lock
Pipfile.lock

It stores:
Exact dependency versions
Checksums
Integrity verification

Example:
chi/v5 v5.0.10
checksum abc123...

This ensures:
My machine
Your machine
CI/CD server
Docker build

all download the exact same package.

Why do we need both?
==================
go.mod
Declares: What dependencies are required
Example: require github.com/go-chi/chi/v5

go.sum
Records:
Exactly which version was installed and verifies integrity

What is Docker doing here?
============================
Your Compose file:

services:
  proxy:
    image: nginx

➔ Creates: NGINX Container

backend:
  build:
    context: backend

➔ Creates: Go Backend Container

Architecture:
==============

                    Browser
                       |
                       v
               +---------------+
               | NGINX Proxy   |
               | Port 80       |
               +---------------+
                       |
                       v
               +---------------+
               | Go Backend    |
               | Port 80       |
               +---------------+

Why introduce Nginx if Go already serves HTTP?
================================================

Because in real systems Nginx often handles:

➔ Reverse proxy
➔ SSL/TLS termination
➔ Load balancing
➔ Compression
➔ Caching
➔ Routing

Example:
=======
Internet
   |
   v
NGINX
   |
   +----> Go App 1
   |
   +----> Go App 2
   |
   +----> Go App 3

So this project is teaching the same architecture pattern you saw with Node.js:

Client
  |
  v
Reverse Proxy (NGINX)
  |
  v
Application Server (Go)

==============================================================
Technologies:
=============================================================
NGINX      -> Reverse Proxy
Go         -> Backend Application
Chi Router -> HTTP Routing Framework
Docker     -> Containerization
Docker Compose -> Multi-container orchestration

Files:

main.go  -> Application code
go.mod   -> Dependency definitions
go.sum   -> Dependency lock/checksum file
Dockerfile -> Builds Go container
docker-compose.yml -> Runs Nginx + Go together
